<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chama Cha Mapinduzi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as CCM, Party of the Revolution</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chama Cha Mapinduzi (CCM), the Party of the Revolution, is the ruling party of Tanzania, formed on 5 February 1977 by the merger of the mainland's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanganyika-african-national-union">Tanganyika African National Union</a> and Zanzibar's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afro-shirazi-party">Afro-Shirazi Party</a>. It was the sole legal party until 1992 and has won every national election since multi-party politics began, making it one of the longest-ruling parties in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chama-cha-mapinduzi.jpg" alt="Chama Cha Mapinduzi" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SVG: amygdalicGloss Logo: CCM · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Chama_Cha_Mapinduzi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Chama Cha Mapinduzi, literally Party of the Revolution, was created on 5 February 1977 under the leadership of Julius Kambarage Nyerere, regarded as the Founding Father of Tanzania. It merged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanganyika-african-national-union">Tanganyika African National Union</a>, the ruling party in Tanganyika, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afro-shirazi-party">Afro-Shirazi Party</a>, the ruling party in Zanzibar. Tanganyika African National Union and its successor have dominated Tanzanian politics since the independence of Tanganyika in 1961. Through the merger the party also became the ruling party in Zanzibar, where its hold has been more contested since the mid-1990s by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civic-united-front">Civic United Front</a> and later the Alliance for Change and Transparency.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1977 until 1992 it was the only legally permitted party. Every five years its national chairman was automatically elected president, confirmed by referendum, while voters chose between two party candidates for each seat in the National Assembly. This changed on 1 July 1992, when amendments to the Constitution and laws allowing more than one party were enacted.</p><p>Originally a champion of African socialism and of the collectivised agriculture known as Ujamaa, firmly oriented to the left, the party later espoused a more mixed economic approach, with a focus on economic diplomacy abroad. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-magufuli">John Magufuli</a>, president from 2015 until his death in 2021, it moved in a left-conservative direction. It was admitted to the Socialist International as a full member in February 2013.</p><p>The party has won every presidential election under the multi-party system, in 1995, 2000, 2005, 2010, 2015, 2020 and 2025, and has kept a supermajority in the legislature. In 2005 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakaya-kikwete">Jakaya Kikwete</a> won the presidency with 80.28 percent of the vote and the party took 206 of 232 directly elected seats.</p><p>In 2015 Magufuli won with 58 percent, the lowest share claimed by the party or its predecessors. In 2020 he won with over 84 percent, and the party took 256 of 264 constituencies. Critics have described Magufuli's presidency as marked by attacks on the opposition, civil society and the press. Research suggests that nostalgia for the party that brought independence and kept relative peace is a major source of its support, which is strong among subsistence farmers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chama Cha Mapinduzi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1977 the two parties merged to form <b>Chama Cha Mapinduzi</b> (CCM), the ‘Party of the Revolution’, which has held power ever since.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tanzania">Tanzania · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…parties won free elections in Lithuania in 1992, Poland in 1993 and Hungary in 1994, and several former single parties remain in power under multi-party constitutions, among them Tanzania’s <b>Chama Cha Mapinduzi</b>, Mozambique’s FRELIMO and Angola’s MPLA; scholars now study dominant-party and “electoral authoritarian” regimes alongside one-party states.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
